Question 1: Key Differences Between HTTP Versions & HTTP/3 Transport Layer

    HTTP/0.9: The most basic version. It only supported text, used the GET method, had no headers or status codes, and closed the connection right after sending the response.

    HTTP/1.0: Added headers, status codes, and supported different file types (like images). It also introduced methods like POST and HEAD, but each request needed a brand-new TCP connection.

    HTTP/1.1: Introduced persistent connections so multiple requests could use the same TCP connection. It also added chunked transfer and better caching.

    HTTP/2: Moved from text format to binary. It introduced multiplexing (sending multiple requests at the same time over one connection) and header compression.

    HTTP/3: Replaced TCP with QUIC (which runs over UDP). This helps avoid connection delays, speeds up handshakes, and keeps connections alive even if your IP changes.

Why UDP in HTTP/3?
HTTP/3 uses UDP (via QUIC) mainly to fix head-of-line blocking in TCP. In TCP, if a single packet gets lost, everything stops and waits for that packet to resend. UDP allows QUIC to handle loss recovery on a per-stream basis, so one lost packet doesn't slow down the rest of your data.
Question 2: Stateless vs. Stateful Web Communications
A. Stateless vs. Stateful

    Stateless: Every request from a user is completely independent. The server doesn't remember anything about past requests.

    Stateful: The server keeps track of the client’s session history, meaning future requests depend on what happened in earlier ones.

B. HTTP Category and State Management

    HTTP Category: HTTP is stateless by default.

    Solution: To maintain state (like staying logged in or keeping items in a shopping cart), web apps use cookies, sessions, or tokens (like JWT) sent in the request headers.

Question 3: Client-Server Limitations in HTTP and Real-Time Solutions
A. The Challenge

Because HTTP uses a standard client-server model, the client has to start every request. The server can never send data on its own without being asked first. This makes building real-time features like chat apps, webhooks, or live alerts really difficult because the server can't push new events to the user instantly.
B. Solutions

    Polling: The client constantly asks the server for updates every few seconds. It works, but it wastes a lot of network traffic when there’s no new data.

    Long Polling: The client sends a request, and the server holds onto it until new data is ready. Once sent, the client immediately sends another request.

    WebSockets: Creates a two-way, persistent connection between the client and server so both sides can send messages instantly at any time.

    Server-Sent Events (SSE): The client opens one HTTP connection, and the server keeps streaming text updates to the client whenever new data comes in.

