Question 5: REST API HTTP Status Codes

    Website database is down and login requests fail:

        503 Service Unavailable (or 500 Internal Server Error). 503 is best here because it shows the database/server is temporarily down.

    Username entered doesn't exist on the site:

        404 Not Found (if treating the user as a missing resource) or 401 Unauthorized (standard practice for security so hackers can't guess valid usernames).

    Website moved to another domain:

        301 Moved Permanently.

    User sent too many requests (exceeded rate limit):

        429 Too Many Requests.

    Token (JWT) renewal was successful and token returned in header:

        200 OK.

    User is accessing from a banned IP range / sanctioned country:

        403 Forbidden.

Question 6: Proxy Types

    Using university local servers instead of main servers for apt package downloads:

        Caching Proxy (or Local Mirror Proxy) – It stores package files locally so students can download them faster without hitting external servers.

    Routing user requests based on their geographic location:

        Reverse Proxy / Load Balancer – It sits in front of the servers and routes users to the server nearest to them.

    Adding authentication to an old legacy service:

        Forward Proxy / Gateway Proxy – It acts as a middleman in front of the legacy app to handle login/auth before letting traffic pass through.
